A||||6||Tom88gt||strange vaccum valve by the "coffee" can||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||06-29-2001||11:59 AM||||I was replacing some vaccum lines for my cruise control when I noticed a strange vaccum valve that appears to be missing some hoses.<P>It is the valve that is on top of the "coffee can" looking thing in the engine bay.  This coffee can is right below the cruise control unit in the engine bay.  There is an electrical plug attached to this valve.  A vaccum hose goes from the cruise control unit to this vaccum valve.  On the other side of this valve are 3 vaccum hose fittings, but on my car there are no hoses there and I can't see where there are any missing hoses elsewhere.<P>TIA for any insite about this valve.<P>Tom||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||06-29-2001||01:22 PM||||I noticed that on my car too - looks like a hose should be there - but since my cruise works ok and my idle and mileage are good - I dont think anything connects to those 'nipples'<P>  maybe they are used on other cars for something - or for testing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||06-29-2001||01:27 PM||||I believe that is a vacuum "release" valve.  When you turn off your cruise you have to bring the chamber up to atmosheric pressure.  There is a little filter in the valve assembly which I think prevent dirt from outside from entering.  <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>I noticed that on my car too - looks like a hose should be there - but since my cruise works ok and my idle and mileage are good - I dont think anything connects to those 'nipples'<P>  maybe they are used on other cars for something - or for testing.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||06-29-2001||06:49 PM||||Yep, cruise vacuum bleed.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||06-29-2001||07:36 PM||||To expand on Terry's answer. That is a backup release for the cruise. It makes sure that when you step on the brake the cruise releases even if the cruise module keeps the main valves shut or the main valves are defective.<P>The release should only have 2 vacuum lines. The third port should be kept clean and if posible point down so it doesn't collect dirt/water.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||06-29-2001||08:06 PM||||You have no lines on the purge valve.... not a good thing.<P>Most likely someone shunted it because the valve got trash in it. <P>Unfortunatly I don't have one of those valves to tell you exactly how to test it.<P>With engine on. air should flow between only two openings. When you step on the brake air should pass between one of the previous two holes and the third port.<P>The valve probably got crud in there and would not close. You might be able to clean it out with contact cleaner. Other wise you should replace it. It prevents the posibility of the cruse unit hanging up.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tom88gt||07-04-2001||11:43 AM||||The middle nipple has the remnants of a sponge filter.  But the cruise control works, my idle is good and MPG are good.  So I'll probably leave it alone for now.  I was just curious as to what it was.  I thought it was a vacuum bleed/release valve for the cruise control but wasn't sure.||||reg||1
